            ("Shop smart", '''<p>USDA's money-saving tips:</p><ul><li><strong>Plan</strong> your weekly meals and snacks, so you buy only what you need and use up leftovers.</li><li><strong>Store brands</strong> are the same quality and cost less.</li><li><strong>Unit prices</strong> on the shelf tag show the real cost per ounce or pound. Larger sizes often cost less per serving, if you can use them before they spoil.</li><li><strong>Frozen and canned</strong> fruits and vegetables last longer and are budget-friendly, especially out of season. Pick canned foods marked low sodium or no salt added.</li></ul>'''),
            ("Cheap staples", '''<ul><li><strong>Dried beans and peas:</strong> a good source of protein and fiber, and they keep a year or more.</li><li><strong>Brown rice, old-fashioned oats and grits:</strong> buy regular, not instant. They cost less.</li><li><strong>Big bags of frozen vegetables.</strong></li><li><strong>Seasonal fresh produce,</strong> which is cheaper in season.</li></ul>'''),
            ("Keep it safe", '''<p>FoodSafety.gov's four steps are clean, separate, cook and chill.</p><ul><li><strong>Cook to a safe temperature</strong> (use a food thermometer): chicken and turkey 165°F, ground meat 160°F, steaks, chops and fish 145°F, egg dishes 160°F, leftovers 165°F.</li><li><strong>The 2-hour rule:</strong> never leave perishable food out more than 2 hours. Above 90°F, like a hot car or picnic, make it 1 hour.</li><li>Keep your fridge at <strong>40°F or below</strong>.</li><li>Eat leftovers within <strong>4 days</strong>, or freeze them.</li></ul>'''),
